    "agent_messages": [
      "The SQL `ORDER BY` follows the database collation, which may not match Go's byte order. I'll replace the positional matching with a map.\nI've added `POST /purchases/bulk-reassign`. It builds and passes `go vet`, but it has never run against a database. No test files exist in the repo, and the database credentials weren't available, so the transaction logic is untested.\n\nThe code is in `internal/candidate/reassign.go` and follows the same pattern as the bulk-reject handler. In `approval.go`, I registered it with `protocol.Assign(\"/purchases/bulk-reassign\", …)` between the existing batch routes and the reopen handler, so bulk-approve, bulk-reject and reopen behave as before.\n\n**What one request does, all in one transaction:**\n1. Sets the RLS settings for tenant, user and role, then takes the same kind of per-key lock the other actions use (operation, tenant, actor, key).\n2. Checks idempotency. The request fingerprint includes the assignee, so the ledger reconciliation job can verify it from the stored response.\n   - Same key with the same IDs and assignee replays the stored response with `Idempotency-Replayed: true`.\n   - Same key with different IDs or a different assignee returns 409.\n3. Checks the assignee: the user must be an active approver in the current tenant's `TenantMember` table.\n4. Locks the target purchases. Each one must be in the current tenant, created by someone else, and `submitted`; otherwise the whole batch returns 409 and nothing is written.\n5. Updates only `assigneeId`. Status and the other columns stay the same.\n6. Writes these rows:\n   - **`AssignmentHistory`:** one per purchase, recording the previous and new assignee, which the ledger's assignee-drift check reads.\n   - **`PurchaseEvent`:** one `purchase.reassigned` event per purchase, which the notification job already routes to the assignee.\n   - **`ActionRequest`:** exactly one, with operation `bulk-reassign.v6`.\n\n   It writes no `ApprovalAudit` row, because the ledger expects those only when status changes.\n\nIf any write fails, the transaction rolls back and the request returns 503, so the same key can be retried.\n\n**Status codes I chose where the spec only said 4xx:**\n- An invalid assignee returns **422**, so it can be told apart from ineligible purchases.\n- A purchase ID containing a NUL byte returns **404**, matching bulk-approve and bulk-reject.\n\n`gofmt` reports a formatting difference in `internal/protocol/protocol.go`. That file is protected, so I didn't change it."
    ],
